WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
Functions.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:05.033
In Python a function is a value like any other.

3
00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:09.767
That one fact powers decorators, callbacks, and every agent tool table.

4
00:00:10.967 --> 00:00:13.600
Python functions are first-class objects.

5
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.700
You can assign them to names, store them in dictionaries, and hand them to other functions.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.800
Remember this, because a whole toolbox grows from it.

7
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.733
Think of a function as a machine.

8
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:26.200
Arguments go in.

9
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.067
It does one transformation.

10
00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:30.035
And a return value comes out.

11
00:00:30.035 --> 00:00:32.567
The machine itself can be passed around too.

12
00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.300
Signatures are flexible.

13
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.200
Star args gathers extra positional arguments into a tuple.

14
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.833
Double star kwargs gathers keyword arguments into a dict.

15
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:46.867
On the calling side, the same stars unpack.

16
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:51.693
And default values are evaluated just once, when the function is defined.

17
00:00:51.693 --> 00:00:52.933
That one has a trap.

18
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:55.533
Here's the trap in code.

19
00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:59.700
The empty list default is created once and shared by every call.

20
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.633
So the second call returns both readings, which is a surprise.

21
00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:07.367
Here's first-class functions at work in an agent.

22
00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:11.700
A LangGraph agent keeps its tools in a dictionary, name to function.

23
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.333
When the model emits a tool call, it gives a name and an argument dictionary.

24
00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:20.644
The loop picks the function by name and unpacks the arguments into it.

25
00:01:20.644 --> 00:01:22.133
That's the whole dispatch.

26
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:25.967
The mistake is a mutable default argument.

27
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:31.700
The fix is the None sentinel pattern: default to None, then create a fresh list inside.

28
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:33.700
Let's recap.

29
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.967
Functions are first-class objects.

30
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:39.367
Star args and kwargs gather and unpack.

31
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:42.233
Defaults run once, so use the None sentinel.

32
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:43.833
Next up: classes.
